\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We fine-tune Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct on 8K multi-turn conversations from the UltraChat training split. Training uses 3 epochs with AdamW (lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$, batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8, seed 42; ThreadGate additionally with seed 123); each variant requires approximately 6 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (102\,GB). Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}; the controlled comparison design is described in \S\ref{sec:comparison-design}. We evaluate under \emph{IID} (same-distribution UltraChat text) and \emph{held-out} (50 non-overlapping conversations) teacher-forced protocols, reporting GAR-ours, GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}, and PPL.

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:four-way} presents the five-way comparison. Under teacher-forced evaluation, all four fine-tuned variants converge to nearly identical performance: GAR-ours spans only 0.75 percentage points (pp) across configurations (0.50--0.51), and PPL is 2.78 for all four on IID evaluation.

The Base model confirms that LoRA fine-tuning is the operative variable: GAR-ours jumps from 0.01 to ${\sim}$0.50, while PPL improves from 4.23 to 2.78. Since all four variants share the same GAR loss ($\tau = 0.5$), convergence near the target is expected and does not demonstrate gating benefit. We cannot exclude LoRA capacity saturation at rank~16, though uniform convergence across four architecturally distinct variants makes this unlikely. GAR-Dongre remains uniformly low (0.01--0.02) across all standard-trained conditions, consistent with \citet{dongre2026attention}; a preliminary scheduled sampling experiment breaks this pattern (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

Under autoregressive generation ($N$\,=\,50), GAR-ours drops to 0.019--0.064, an 8--17$\times$ gap from teacher-forced performance. Turn-conditioned gating (ThreadGate) provides statistically significant improvement over LoRA-only (Wilcoxon $p$\,=\,0.004, $r$\,=\,0.454; GAR-ours 0.064 vs 0.037), yet the residual gap remains large (7.8$\times$), confirming exposure bias as the dominant barrier rather than gating architecture. A second ThreadGate seed (123) confirms TF stability: GAR-ours $0.504\pm0.001$, identical PPL. We analyze this distribution shift in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Five-way comparison on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct. Teacher-forced (TF) evaluation uses IID UltraChat text and 50 held-out conversations; autoregressive (AR) evaluation uses $N$\,=\,50 held-out conversations (mean 3.6 user turns). All fine-tuned variants converge under TF (0.75\,pp spread); the 8--17$\times$ TF-AR gap persists across all configurations. ThreadGate significantly outperforms LoRA-only under AR (Wilcoxon $p$\,=\,0.004, $r$\,=\,0.454) but the residual gap remains large. Base is the pretrained model; all other rows share the same LoRA configuration, optimizer, and training data. GAR-O = GAR-ours; GAR-D = GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}.}
\label{tab:four-way}
\begin{tabular}{l ccc ccc cc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{IID (TF)}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{Held-out (TF)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{AR ($N$\,=\,50)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-7} \cmidrule(lr){8-9}
\textbf{Configuration} & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL & GAR-O & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
Base              & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.23 & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.34 & 0.019 & {\scriptsize[.014,\,.024]} \\
\midrule
ThreadGate        & 0.50 & \textbf{0.02} & 2.78 & 0.50 & \textbf{0.02} & 2.78 & \textbf{0.064} & {\scriptsize[.041,\,.091]} \\
\;+\,PCGrad       & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.030 & {\scriptsize[.023,\,.038]} \\
Uniform Gate      & \textbf{0.51} & 0.01 & 2.78 & \textbf{0.51} & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.033 & {\scriptsize[.025,\,.043]} \\
LoRA-only         & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.037 & {\scriptsize[.027,\,.050]} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_attention_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-turn GAR-ours under teacher-forced evaluation ($N$\,=\,45, $\pm$1\,std). Fine-tuned variants maintain GAR near $\tau$\,=\,0.5 with 7--8\% decay (T1$\to$T4); Base decays 80\%. T5 std inflated by truncated conversations. Despite this preservation, autoregressive GAR collapses to 0.019--0.064 for all variants (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}), consistent with distribution shift as the primary failure mode under these conditions.}
  \label{fig:attention-heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

All training-time interventions leave the exposure bias gap largely intact. We test whether inference-time attention manipulation can recover GAR by applying an additive bias $\beta$ to goal-token attention logits at every layer of the ThreadGate checkpoint during generation.

An initial sweep on $N$\,=\,5 ten-turn conversations (Figure~\ref{fig:steering}a) reveals a sharp nonlinearity between $\beta$\,=\,2 and $\beta$\,=\,5: GAR increases ${\sim}$11$\times$ (0.062\,$\to$\,0.6795$\pm$0.020) while PPL rises 35\% (1.41\,$\to$\,1.91). At $\beta$\,=\,10, GAR saturates at 0.959 but PPL degrades to 4.22.

$N$\,=\,50 conversations from a held-out evaluation set (39 with $\geq$3 turns, mean 3.6 user turns; Figure~\ref{fig:steering}b) confirm the qualitative steering pattern observed in the $N$\,=\,5 pilot (10 turns each), with consistent nonlinearity range and saturation behavior. A finer $\beta$ sweep localizes the nonlinearity: GAR jumps from 0.128$\pm$0.110 ($\beta$\,=\,2) to 0.556$\pm$0.060 ($\beta$\,=\,3), an 8.7$\times$ recovery from baseline with only 9\% PPL increase (1.43\,$\to$\,1.56), identifying $\beta$\,=\,3 as a practical operating point. The $\beta$\,=\,0 distribution is right-skewed (mean 0.064, median 0.031, std 0.081), driven by short conversations with high baseline GAR; absolute baselines therefore differ from the 10-turn $N$\,=\,5 evaluation (0.028). At $\beta$\,=\,5, GAR reaches 0.727$\pm$0.061 with PPL 1.77$\pm$0.27, confirming the recovery pattern at scale.

These results provide constructive evidence that attention misallocation---not representation degradation---is a proximal cause of exposure-bias-induced GAR failure under the tested conditions (Qwen-2.5-7B, UltraChat, GAR-family losses): manually correcting attention via $\beta$ bias restores GAR, and inference-time intervention circumvents the exposure bias barrier. This steering experiment is diagnostic: it requires identifying goal tokens, trivial for system prompts but not generalizable.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_attention_steering.pdf}
  \caption{Inference-time attention steering: additive bias $\beta$ applied to goal-token attention logits during autoregressive generation (error bars show $\pm$1\,std). (a)~Initial evaluation ($N$\,=\,5, 10-turn conversations): sharp increase at $\beta \in [2, 5]$, GAR ${\sim}$11$\times$ recovery with 35\% PPL rise. (b)~Larger-scale evaluation ($N$\,=\,50, 39 with $\geq$3 turns, mean 3.6 user turns) with finer $\beta$ sweep: the nonlinearity localizes between $\beta$\,=\,2 and $\beta$\,=\,3, identifying $\beta$\,=\,3 as a practical operating point (8.7$\times$ GAR recovery, 9\% PPL cost). High-$\beta$ results are consistent across sample sizes. Dotted line: unsteered teacher-forced baseline.}
  \label{fig:steering}
\end{figure}

